          "focus": "Determine the precise contextual meaning of a specific word or phrase",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 13 · Q1",
          "passageTitle": "Words in Context - Synonyms — Q1",
          "passage": "(*Exam level)\nThe following text is adapted from Anton\nChekhov's 1904 playThe Cherry Orchard\n(translated by Julius West in 1916).\nTROFIMOV: Believe me, Anya, believe me! I'm\nnot thirty yet, I'm young, I'm still a student, but\nI haveundergone a great deal! I'm as hungry as\nthe winter, I'm ill, I'm shaken..and where\nhaven't I been- -fate has tossed me everywhere!",
          "prompt": "As used in the text, what does the word\n\"undergone\" most nearly mean?",
          "options": [
            "Neglected",
            "Enjoyed",
            "Conveyed",
            "Endured"
          ],
          "answer": 3,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is D (Answer Key: Topic 13, Q1). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t13-q002",
          "skill": "Words in Context - Synonyms",
          "domain": "Craft and Structure",
          "focus": "Determine the precise contextual meaning of a specific word or phrase",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 13 · Q2",
          "passageTitle": "Words in Context - Synonyms — Q2",
          "passage": "The following text is adapted from John\nMatheus's 1926 short story, \"Mr. Bradford\nTeaches Sunday School.\" Mr. Bradford is\ndriving through the countryside in Florida.\nThe moss in the towering water oaks had\nbecome enlivened with a verdant sheen of silver\nand hung like festoons of carnival or like funeral\ndecorations for the mourning of the dead. The\npine cones were pungent, the pine green was\nresplendent. The bald cypresses spread\nthemselves along the water courses while the\nwillows wept as they always did. Mr. Bradford\nwas conscious of this gorgeousdisplay of nature.",
          "prompt": "As used in the text, what does the word\n\"display\" most nearly mean?",
          "options": [
            "Spectacle",
            "Reproduction",
            "Pretentiousness",
            "Disguise"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is A (Answer Key: Topic 13, Q2). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t13-q003",
          "skill": "Words in Context - Synonyms",
          "domain": "Craft and Structure",
          "focus": "Determine the precise contextual meaning of a specific word or phrase",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 13 · Q3",
          "passageTitle": "Words in Context - Synonyms — Q3",
          "passage": "The following text is adapted from Kenneth\nGrahame's 1908 novelThe Wind in the Willows.\nThe Mole is dazed after briefly meeting a\nstranger while traveling with a friend.\n(The) Mole stood still a moment, held in\nthought. As one wakenedsuddenly from a\nbeautiful dream, who struggles to recall it, and\ncan re-capture nothing but a dim sense of the\nbeauty of it, the beauty! Till that, too, fades\naway in its turn.",
          "prompt": "As used in the text, what does the word\n\"suddenly\" most nearly mean?",
          "options": [
            "Abruptly",
            "Repeatedly",
            "Voluntarily",
            "Tenderly"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is A (Answer Key: Topic 13, Q3). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t13-q004",
          "skill": "Words in Context - Synonyms",
          "domain": "Craft and Structure",
          "focus": "Determine the precise contextual meaning of a specific word or phrase",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 13 · Q4",
          "passageTitle": "Words in Context - Synonyms — Q4",
          "passage": "The following text is from Mark Twain's 1876\nnovel The Adventures of Tom Sawyer. Tom, a\nchild, has been told by his aunt to paint their\nhouse's fence.\nTom appeared on the sidewalk with a bucket of\nwhitewash and a long-handled brush. He\nsurveyed the fence, and all gladness left him and\na deep melancholy settled down upon his spirit.\nThirty yards of board fence nine feet high.",
          "prompt": "As used in the text, what does the word\n\"surveyed\" most nearly mean?",
          "options": [
            "Complimented",
            "Examined",
            "Converted",
            "Organized"
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is B (Answer Key: Topic 13, Q4). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t13-q005",
          "skill": "Words in Context - Synonyms",
          "domain": "Craft and Structure",
          "focus": "Determine the precise contextual meaning of a specific word or phrase",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 13 · Q5",
          "passageTitle": "Words in Context - Synonyms — Q5",
          "passage": "The following text is from Lilliam Rivera's 2020\nnovel Never Look Back. The text describes the\nnarrator looking around his father's apartment.\nPops's pride and joy is his wall of towering\nbookshelves. He once said you cantell a man's\nworth by the number of books he owns. I guess\nthis means Pops is overflowing with riches\nbecause ever since I can remember I was never\nwithout a book within reach.",
          "prompt": "As used in the text, what does the word \"tell'\nmost nearly mean?",
          "options": [
            "Judge",
            "Urge",
            "Adore",
            "Teach"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is A (Answer Key: Topic 13, Q5). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t13-q006",
          "skill": "Words in Context - Synonyms",
          "domain": "Craft and Structure",
          "focus": "Determine the precise contextual meaning of a specific word or phrase",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 13 · Q6",
          "passageTitle": "Words in Context - Synonyms — Q6",
          "passage": "The following text is from Virginia Woolf's 1919\nnovel Night and Day. The text describes a\ngathering of aspiring young artists and\nintellectuals.\nOne person after another rose, and, as with an\nill-balanced axe, attempted tohew out his\nconception of art a little more clearly, and sat\ndown with the feeling that, for some reason\nwhich he could not grasp, his strokes had gone\nawry. As they sat down they turned almost\ninvariably to the person sitting next them, and\nrectified and continued what they had just said\nin public.",
          "prompt": "As used in the text, what does the figurative\nphrase \"hew out\" most nearly mean?",
          "options": [
            "Visualize",
            "Emulate",
            "Vacate",
            "Define"
          ],
          "answer": 3,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is D (Answer Key: Topic 13, Q6). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t13-q007",
          "skill": "Words in Context - Synonyms",
          "domain": "Craft and Structure",
          "focus": "Determine the precise contextual meaning of a specific word or phrase",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 13 · Q7",
          "passageTitle": "Words in Context - Synonyms — Q7",
          "passage": "The following text is from Rudolfo Anaya's 1972\nnovel Bless Me, Ultima.The young narrator is\neating breakfast with his two sisters in their\nhome in rural New Mexico.\nI sat across the table from Deborah and Teresa\nand ate my atole and the hot tortilla with\nbutter. I said very little. I usually spoke very\nlittle to my two sisters. They were older than I\nand they were very close. They usually spent\nthe entire day in the attic, playing dolls and\ngiggling. I did notconcern myself with those\nthings.",
          "prompt": "As used in the text, what does the phrase\n\"concern myself with\" most nearly mean?",
          "options": [
            "craft",
